'''Sartan Guajicutea''' (1609–1723) was the colonial administration of [[Guajicutea]] under [[Sarta|Sartan]] sovereignty, established after the [[Sarta-Guajicutea War]] (1608–1609) deposed [[Carlos I of Guajicutea|Boqor Carlos I]] and ended the [[Kingdom of Guajicutea]]. The colony lasted 114 years, governed by a succession of crown-appointed governors, a peasant interregnum, and a military administrator, before being peacefully transferred to the [[Padres de Guajicutea]] in 1723 to establish the [[Republic of Guajicutea]].

The colony's history was shaped by a trade embargo and famines (1610–1617), the arrival of [[Bayani]] migrants, the construction of the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] and [[Castillo de San Esteban]], a period of cultural fusion between Sartan and Guaji traditions, the [[Sarta-Volisania War]] (1683–1705) which devastated the colonial economy, the [[Interregnum of 1691]] in which the governor was killed by a peasant mob, and the post-war influx of Sartan elites that ultimately drove the republican movement.

== Establishment (1609) ==

The [[Sarta-Guajicutea War]] (1608–1609) ended the [[Kingdom of Guajicutea]]. Sarta sent a fleet, captured [[Tabakurí]], and deposed [[Carlos I of Guajicutea|Boqor Carlos I]]. The Sarta Colony of Guajicutea was established in 1609 with a major restructuring of colonial institutions.

All sixteen feudal bikabás were purged and replaced with Sartan provincial administrators. The [[Burawi Catholic Church|Burawi Catholic]] and [[Petran Catholic|Volisanian Petran Catholic]] churches were expelled, replaced by the Sartan Petran Catholic hierarchy. Slavery was considered and rejected; instead, a laissez-faire labor policy courted foreign diaspora, particularly [[Bayani]] migrants persecuted in [[Balisca]] (a Volisanian province). A strict language policy required Sartan for all religious and commercial activity. A large bureaucracy and military presence was established, as Guajicutea was Sarta's first and only colony.

== Trade embargo and famines (1610–1617) ==

The [[Treaty of 1610]] ended the [[War of the Mesogean]] between [[Burawa]] and [[Volisania]]. Both powers resented Sarta's acquisition of Guajicutea and imposed a complete trade embargo. Sarta was forced to resupply the colony from [[Breisland|Breislandic]] [[Lurandia]], approximately 1,500 km away, with no intermediate ports.

The embargo contributed to two famines: the famine of 1612 and the more severe famine of 1617, exacerbated by deforestation-driven soil erosion. In 1617, the Sartan Petran Church negotiated with Volisania through the shared Petran Catholic tradition, securing Volisanian aid and a resumption of trade. Volisania did not recognize Sartan sovereignty, and Burawa did not participate, but the embargo was broken.

== Population and migration ==

Despite the famines, the colony's population grew rapidly. An arrival cap of 500 per year was imposed in 1618. The population reached approximately 60,000 by 1630, nearly double the pre-colonial peak of approximately 40,000 (c. 1400 CE). Growth was driven by [[Bayani]] migration, Sartan colonization, and natural increase among the Guaji population.

By 1630, the population was approximately 43,000–45,000 Guaji, 8,000–10,000 Bayani, and 5,000–7,000 Sartan colonial personnel. By 1683, the population had reached approximately 80,000.

== Naval and military infrastructure ==

The [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] (Royal Shipyard of Waribá) was established in 1638, initially as a repair facility to reduce dependence on the 1,500 km Lurandia supply line. It was expanded in 1651 with full shipbuilding capacity, merging Sartan shipwright techniques with the Guaji [[Yabikú]] joinery tradition. The shipyard became the largest industrial installation in the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}}.

The [[Castillo de San Esteban]] (Castle of Saint Stephen) was a citadel fort complex on the promontory overlooking [[Waribá]] harbor, constructed between 1642 and 1656. With stone walls, four bastions, and a garrison of approximately 500, it was the largest fortification in the Capuyaquiran Sea. Guaji stoneworkers — descendants of the catacomb corridor craftsmen — applied their skills to the citadel's dressed stone and carved details.

== Cultural fusion ==

Over decades of colonial rule, a fusion of Guaji and Sartan traditions produced a distinct colonial society. Sartan became the dominant language of urban areas, trade, religion, and government, while the Guaji language survived in rural communities. A syncretic Guaji Catholicism absorbed indigenous spiritual elements — the triad of sea, mountain, and sky mapped onto the Holy Trinity — into the Petran Catholic framework. Cremation, practiced since 516 CE, persisted in hybrid form: a Catholic funeral rite followed by cremation rather than interment.

The first written literature by Guaji-born authors in Sartan emerged during this period, including the transcription of the [[Kari Yabí]] chronicle into Sartan circa the 1650s. The lunar calendar, officially replaced by the solar calendar, survived in rural agricultural and fishing communities.

== Sarta-Volisania War (1683–1705) ==

{{main|Sarta-Volisania War}}

The [[Sarta-Volisania War]] (1683–1705) was fought primarily in the Old World. In Guajicutea, the impact was economic and political. Volisanian trade ended. The [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] shifted to galleon production but trickled to a halt as Sarta ran out of money. The colony became increasingly isolated from the mainland.

The colonial administration declared martial law in 1685, imposed curfews, increased taxation, and instituted food requisitioning in 1687. Multiple peasant revolts — the [[Waribá Food Riots]] (1686), [[Southern Peasant Revolt]] (1688–1689), and [[Tabakurí Uprising]] (1690) — weakened the governor's authority and forced the collapse of the plantation system.

In 1691, Governor [[Felipe Ortega]] was overthrown and killed by a peasant mob in Waribá. During the [[Interregnum of 1691]], a peasant ruler known as [[Waraku]] seized power for three months. A Burawi fleet from [[Lacashe]] attempted to retake the island but was pushed out by local chaos. Order was restored in 1692 by a [[Breisland]]-[[Mercia]] alliance, which installed [[Captain Andrés Caldera]] as temporary administrator.

The Treaty of 1705 ended the war. Sarta was bankrupted and landlocked, its fleets destroyed. The treaty forced Sarta to cede the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] to the [[Companyia Reial del Capuyaquiran]], a Volisanian royal trading company.

== Post-war and peaceful revolution (1705–1723) ==

{{main|History of Guajicutea (1705-1723)}}

The post-war colony experienced an influx of Sartan elites fleeing the devastated mainland, driven by a royal land sales program. The [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716) between [[Volisania]] and [[Zong]] enriched the Companyia Reial and opened trade routes to Zong territories, flooding the colony with cheap goods that killed the local cotton industry and forced a shift to sugar, tobacco, and dyes.

A republican movement, influenced by {{w|Age of Enlightenment|Enlightenment}} ideas from southern [[Illypnia]] and the [[Commonwealth of Louyang]] model, culminated in the [[Peaceful revolution of 1723|Peaceful Revolution of 1723]]. [[Don Vicente de la Vega]], a former Sartan nobleman turned large landowner, convinced the king of Sarta to cede the colony to the [[Padres de Guajicutea]], who established the [[Republic of Guajicutea]].

